\section*{The Lantern on the Hill}

10ICEN04

by the poet of this reader

The evening folds the valley slow,
and one by one the windows glow;
above the fields, against the dark,
a lantern climbs the hillside stark.

Who carries it, we cannot say —
a shepherd late, a child at play —
yet every eye along the lane
is drawn to that unhurried flame.

It does not race, it does not rest,
it picks the stony pathway best;
and when it crests the final stone,
the whole dark hill seems less alone.

So keep your small light burning true
through every field you wander through;
the valley watches, though you climb —
one lantern lifts the whole night's time.

\section*{About the poem}

The poem watches a single light crossing a dark hillside and finds in it a
figure for quiet perseverance. The speaker never learns who carries the
lantern; the comfort lies in the light itself.

\section*{Comprehension}

1. What time of day does the first stanza describe? Pick two phrases that
tell you.
2. Why do you think the poet calls the flame "unhurried"?
3. What does the lantern seem to mean to the people in the lane?
4. Explain the line "one lantern lifts the whole night's time" in your own
words.

\section*{Appreciation}

1. Find two pairs of rhyming words from the second stanza.
2. The lantern is a symbol. What does it stand for? Give one reason from
the poem.
